      {
        "rank": 2,
        "kind": "cache-expiry",
        "explanation": "After a pause longer than the cache lifetime the provider's prompt cache expired, and the whole context was written to the cache again at the write price instead of being read at the read price.",
        "action": "Before a long pause, finish the task and start the next one in a fresh session, or compact (`/compact`) so that less has to be written again. Long-idle sessions with a large context are the expensive ones.",
        "count": 1,
        "total": {
          "count": 20500,
          "measured": true
        },
        "share_of_baseline": null,
        "share_of_total_tokens": 0.03906860446944835,
        "items": [
          {
            "name": "cache_expiry",
            "tokens": {
              "count": 20500,
              "measured": true
            },
            "requests": 1,
            "detail": "1 request after a pause of 30 min, 20.5k tokens written again, TTL 5 min",
            "session_id": "cache_expiry"
          }
        ],
        "notes": [
          "A request counts when it came more than the session's cache lifetime after the previous main-agent request and wrote more tokens than it read. The saving is those written tokens, in USD the difference between the write and the read price of the request's own model. It is inferred: the action shrinks the rewrite, it does not prove that it would not have happened.",
          "The lifetime is 5 minutes, or 60 minutes for a session in which a request wrote tokens with the 1-hour lifetime. It is read from the client's own report where the client gives one (Claude Code) and assumed to be 5 minutes elsewhere.",
          "The Context size saving counts the volume of a large context, this finding the price difference of writing it again. Both can name the same session; their savings are not additive."
        ],
        "impact": {
          "saving": {
            "count": 20500,
            "measured": true
          },
